<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dispositio Achillea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1473">1473</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dispositio Achillea was the house law issued in 1473 by Albert <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a>, Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, to regulate the Hohenzollern succession. It laid down that the Mark of Brandenburg with the electoral dignity should pass undivided to the eldest son, while the Franconian lands around Ansbach and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a> could go to younger sons. It helped keep Brandenburg from being partitioned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dispositio-achillea.jpg" alt="Dispositio Achillea" width="190" height="337" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Känsterle at Dutch Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AlbrechtAchilles.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Also known as the Constitutio Achillea or the Achillean House Law, the disposition was set out in the last will and testament of Elector Albert III Achilles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>. The Mark Brandenburg was to stay undivided and pass whole to the eldest son, who at Albert's death would be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-cicero">John Cicero</a>. This matched a requirement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull</a>, which held that electorates must be indivisible.</p><p>The younger sons, Frederick I and Siegmund, were to inherit the Franconian margraviates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-ansbach">Brandenburg-Ansbach</a> and Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulmbach">Kulmbach</a>, later called Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a>, and to draw lots to decide which of them took which principality. Any further sons were to receive no land and to pursue an ecclesiastical career, though Albert had no more.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Strictly, the disposition settled only how the lands of one margrave were to be shared among his sons. In time, however, it came to be accepted as a principle of succession in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a>, and in 1541 it was confirmed, together with the House Treaty of Regensburg, as a binding house law of the dynasty. Its central rule, the indivisibility of the Mark Brandenburg, thus became part of the family's own law and no longer rested only on a clause of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull</a>.</p><p>The settlement also laid the ground for the gradual separation of the Mark Brandenburg from the Hohenzollern family's original lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>. The two margraviates it created there, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-ansbach">Brandenburg-Ansbach</a> and Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulmbach">Kulmbach</a>, were reunited with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, the successor state of the Mark, only in 1791.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dispositio Achillea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Albert Achilles laid down in the <b>Dispositio Achillea</b> of 1473 that the Mark itself should pass undivided to the eldest son.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
